# Line endings, decided here rather than left to each contributor's git configuration.
#
# This matters more than it usually does. A .bat or .cmd file is parsed line by line as it
# runs, and cmd.exe mis-splits bare LF: a script whose first line is "setlocal" fails with
# "'tlocal' is not recognized as an internal or external command". The script is not wrong,
# nothing in it changed, and the error names a command nobody wrote. Measured on this
# repository, not imagined — every batch file in the tree had drifted to LF and the native
# build stopped working until they were converted back.
#
# So Windows script files are pinned to CRLF in the working tree whatever core.autocrlf says,
# and everything else is normalised to LF in the repository so a diff never turns into a
# whole-file rewrite because somebody's editor disagreed.

* text=auto

# Must be CRLF on disk: cmd.exe cannot reliably parse them otherwise.
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf

# PowerShell tolerates LF, but these sit beside the batch files and are edited in the same
# breath; keeping them consistent avoids a confusing mixed-ending directory.
*.ps1   text eol=crlf

# Visual Studio rewrites these with CRLF whenever it touches them, so declaring it prevents a
# spurious full-file diff the first time somebody opens the solution.
*.sln       text eol=crlf
*.csproj    text eol=crlf
*.vcxproj   text eol=crlf

# Source and documentation: LF in the repository, native in the working tree.
*.cs    text
*.cpp   text
*.h     text
*.md    text
*.yml   text
*.xaml  text
*.def   text

# Binary. Never normalised, never diffed as text.
*.ico   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.bmp   binary
*.dll   binary
*.exe   binary
*.ds    binary
